WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.037
The ranking stage groups records using normalized email and normalized name.

00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:10.737
Within each candidate duplicate group, it calculates how complete each record is.

00:00:10.737 --> 00:00:19.347
A usable name, email, valid-shaped phone, and parsed signup date each contribute to that completeness logic.

00:00:19.347 --> 00:00:21.911
The more complete record is ranked first.

00:00:22.545 --> 00:00:28.657
If two records have the same completeness, the identifier is used as a stable tie-breaker.

00:00:28.657 --> 00:00:33.757
That final tie-breaker matters because it makes the result deterministic.

00:00:33.757 --> 00:00:39.262
If you run the query again on the same data, you should receive the same ranking.

00:00:39.262 --> 00:00:43.842
Remember that rank one means preferred by our selected policy.

00:00:43.842 --> 00:00:49.207
It does not prove that the record is factually superior in every possible way.

00:00:49.207 --> 00:00:54.767
The ranking is a reproducible decision rule, not a statement of absolute truth.
